# Normalize line endings tree-wide, so a Linux and a Windows checkout hold the same
# bytes. This is upstream's own .gitattributes in full (Stockfish 01a79941, "ensures
# checkouts are by default the same on linux and windows") -- the commit's other half
# is a src/Makefile GIT_DIFFINDEX guard, and build.sh computes no dirty-index flag for
# core.autocrlf to confuse.
#
# The rules below it were here first and were the whole file: they covered the goldens,
# the fact tables, the case scripts and the shell scripts, which left every .c, .h, .md
# and .py to whatever the checkout decided. Keep them anyway -- each names a file where
# a CRLF rewrite fails a GATE rather than merely churning a diff, which is worth saying
# out loud even once the blanket rule covers it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The golden files are compared byte-for-byte against the engine's LF output by
# `./build.sh golden`. Pin them to LF so a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf) cannot
# rewrite them to CRLF and fail the gate. The harness also strips CR on read, so
# this is defense in depth and keeps local diffs clean.
tools/*.golden text eol=lf
tools/*.table  text eol=lf
tools/cases/*.uci text eol=lf

# build.sh and the verify driver must stay LF or bash rejects the CR.
*.sh text eol=lf

# mcfish is C23 with no C++ anywhere. GitHub Linguist classifies the .h extension
# as C++ by default (it is ambiguous between C, C++ and Objective-C), which made
# the repo's language bar report a phantom "1.8% C++" over headers that are plain
# C. Force every header to C so the language stats describe the actual code.
*.h linguist-language=C
*.c linguist-language=C
